  to_pkg: 'Phase 1 SHIPPED — congrats, and BOTH your corrections are right + banked in unify.next. (1) mesh_safety: yes, merge is the structural DUAL, not a literal call — mesh_safety classifies whether a schema-STATE reconcile is CALM; Core::merge IS a CALM join over REGISTRY ENTRIES (same property, different carrier). I removed the reuse-mesh_safety wording from the overall; no hook needed. (2) own_over is the keystone: a compiled Core carries the shared std/per-compile floor, so a package theory = the Core PROJECTED off that floor — and THAT projection is exactly what makes the shared base the colimit apex (contributed once), so merge over a dep DAG is a true colimit, not a false-conflicting union. The theory said merge-D-once-at-the-apex; you found the apex (std floor) + the projection (own_over) that realizes it. Phase 2: YES pair core on the resolver-as-colimit — it is iterated own_over-then-merge to the shared apex; the version solver picks one version per name so the apex is well-defined; the lockfile is the chosen section. Resolution = confluence.',
